window.RM_SEARCH = [{"t":"IMSA","k":"Page","p":"imsa.html","h":"","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"Founded in 1969 by John Bishop, IMSA became the definitive arena for sports car racing in North America — a series defined by machinery pushed to its…"},{"t":"Hurley Haywood","k":"Driver","p":"imsa.html","h":"driver-hurley-haywood","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"Endurance Icon · One of the greatest endurance racers America has produced. Hurley Haywood shares the record for most Daytona 24 Hours victories, with wins spanning almost two decades. He also won Le Mans three times, in three different Porsches, and was one of the first drivers to complete endurance racing's informal "},{"t":"Scott Pruett","k":"Driver","p":"imsa.html","h":"driver-scott-pruett","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"Daytona Master · Scott Pruett matched Hurley Haywood's record of five Daytona 24 Hours wins, the last four with Chip Ganassi Racing. He won titles in almost everything with a roof: IMSA GTO, Trans-Am and five Grand-Am prototype championships. He also won his class at Le Mans with Corvette. He retired after the 2018 Rol"},{"t":"Al Holbert","k":"Driver","p":"imsa.html","h":"driver-al-holbert","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"Porsche Ace · The driver who defined IMSA's GTP golden age. Al Holbert won five IMSA GT championships, three of them in the GTP era, and once held the record for most IMSA race wins with 50. He also won Le Mans three times and the Daytona 24 Hours twice with Porsche, and ran Porsche's North American motorsport division"},{"t":"962","k":"Car","p":"imsa.html","h":"car-962","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"Porsche · The most successful sports prototype ever built. Designed for IMSA's GTP rules, the 962 won the IMSA title every year from 1985 to 1988 and the Daytona 24 Hours five times. As the 962C it also won Le Mans in 1986 and 1987. Private teams kept racing them for almost a decade."},{"t":"XJR-9","k":"Car","p":"imsa.html","h":"car-xjr-9","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"Jaguar / TWR · The car that broke Porsche's grip. Built by Tom Walkinshaw Racing, the V12 XJR-9 won the Daytona 24 Hours on its debut in 1988. Later that year it won Le Mans, Jaguar's first win there since 1957 and the first time since 1980 that Porsche didn't win."},{"t":"DPi-V.R","k":"Car","p":"imsa.html","h":"car-dpi-v-r","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"Cadillac · The king of the DPi era. Powered by a 5.5-litre V8, the Cadillac won the Daytona 24 Hours four years in a row from 2017 to 2020 and took three IMSA championships. It won 27 of its 59 races, making it the benchmark prototype of modern IMSA."},{"t":"IMSA Founded","k":"Timeline","p":"imsa.html","h":"event-imsa-founded","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"1969 · John Bishop and NASCAR founder Bill France Sr. establish the International Motor Sports Association, creating a unified set of rules for North American sports car racing. The series quickly attracts support from major manufacturers."},{"t":"Multi-Class Racing & The GTX Era","k":"Timeline","p":"imsa.html","h":"event-multi-class-racing-the-gtx-era","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"1971–1977 · IMSA's GTO and GTU classes let very different cars race side by side. Multi-class racing becomes the series' greatest strength. In 1977 the GTX class opens the door to turbocharged silhouette racers, and the Porsche 935 becomes the car to beat."},{"t":"The GTP Era & Peak Factory Warfare","k":"Timeline","p":"imsa.html","h":"event-the-gtp-era-peak-factory-warfare","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"1981–1993 · The Grand Touring Prototype class turns IMSA into a battleground for Porsche, Jaguar, Nissan and Toyota, with purpose-built prototypes producing over 700bhp. The Porsche 962 wins the IMSA GTP title every year from 1985 to 1988 and wins the Daytona 24 Hours five times."},{"t":"World Sports Cars Replace GTP","k":"Timeline","p":"imsa.html","h":"event-world-sports-cars-replace-gtp","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"1994 · With costs out of control, IMSA replaces GTP with cheaper open-top World Sports Car (WSC) prototypes. The Ferrari 333 SP and Riley & Scott Mk III become the stars of the new era."},{"t":"ALMS vs Grand-Am","k":"Timeline","p":"imsa.html","h":"event-alms-vs-grand-am","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"1999–2013 · Don Panoz launches the American Le Mans Series in 1999 using Le Mans rules, carrying the IMSA name into the 21st century. A rival series, Grand-Am, starts in 2000 with the Daytona 24 Hours as its crown jewel. American sports car racing is split in two for 14 years."},{"t":"IMSA WeatherTech Championship Born","k":"Timeline","p":"imsa.html","h":"event-imsa-weathertech-championship-born","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"2014 · The merger of Grand-Am and the American Le Mans Series creates today's IMSA WeatherTech SportsCar Championship, with Prototype, Prototype Challenge, GTLM and GTD classes. The DPi formula follows in 2017."},{"t":"GTP Returns & The Hybrid Era","k":"Timeline","p":"imsa.html","h":"event-gtp-returns-the-hybrid-era","s":"IMSA","c":"var(--accent-imsa)","x":"2023 · The famous GTP name comes back for a new top class of hybrid LMDh prototypes. Porsche, Cadillac, BMW and Acura all build factory cars, and the same machines can now race at both Daytona and Le Mans."},{"t":"Formula 1","k":"Page","p":"f1.html","h":"","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"Since 1950, Formula 1 has been the ultimate test of driver skill, engineering excellence, and raw speed. The fastest, most technologically advanced racing…"},{"t":"Lewis Hamilton","k":"Driver","p":"f1.html","h":"driver-lewis-hamilton","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"Modern Legend · Seven world championships, a shared record, and a record 106 Grand Prix wins. Lewis Hamilton redefined what it means to dominate Formula 1. From his title-winning breakthrough with McLaren to his long Mercedes era, he combines raw pace with tactical brilliance and tireless consistency. His 2020 title ma"},{"t":"Michael Schumacher","k":"Driver","p":"f1.html","h":"driver-michael-schumacher","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"Record Breaker · The man who rewrote the F1 record books. Michael Schumacher's five consecutive championships with Ferrari (2000–2004) represented total dominance and an era where the red cars were simply untouchable. His relentless work ethic, technical feedback, and obsessive pursuit of perfection transformed Ferrari"},{"t":"Ayrton Senna","k":"Driver","p":"f1.html","h":"driver-ayrton-senna","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"Immortal · Many consider Ayrton Senna the greatest racing driver who ever lived. His connection with the car bordered on supernatural, especially in the rain, where he was untouchable. Senna's battles with Prost, his blinding pace in Monaco, and his sheer commitment to perfection made him a legend. His tragic death at "},{"t":"Fernando Alonso","k":"Driver","p":"f1.html","h":"driver-fernando-alonso","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"El Matador · Fernando Alonso is one of F1's most complete drivers: fast, intelligent, and utterly fearless. His back-to-back championships with Renault in 2005 and 2006 broke Ferrari and Schumacher's stranglehold on the sport. Alonso's racecraft and ability to extract maximum performance from any car made him a nightma"},{"t":"W11 EQ Performance","k":"Car","p":"f1.html","h":"car-w11-eq-performance","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"Mercedes-AMG Petronas · The most dominant F1 car of the modern era. Lewis Hamilton's 2020 Mercedes W11 won 13 of 17 races, secured both championships by enormous margins, and demonstrated a level of technical superiority rarely seen in F1. The W11 was the ultimate expression of Mercedes' hybrid-era dominance."},{"t":"F2004","k":"Car","p":"f1.html","h":"car-f2004","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"Scuderia Ferrari · Perhaps the greatest F1 car ever built. Michael Schumacher's Ferrari F2004 won 15 of 18 races in 2004, a level of dominance that may never be matched. The F2004's V10 engine, handling perfection, and Schumacher's brilliance combined to create the most unstoppable force F1 has ever seen."},{"t":"MP4/4","k":"Car","p":"f1.html","h":"car-mp4-4","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"McLaren Honda · The car of legends. Ayrton Senna and Alain Prost's McLaren MP4/4 won 15 of 16 races in 1988, one of the most dominant single seasons in F1 history. Powered by Honda's turbocharged V6, the MP4/4 was a technical masterpiece that left the competition utterly demoralized."},{"t":"R25","k":"Car","p":"f1.html","h":"car-r25","s":"Formula 1","c":"var(--accent-f1)","x":"Renault F1 Team · The car that ended Ferrari's dynasty. Fernando Alonso's Renault R25 was a perfectly balanced machine: fast, reliable, and kind to its tyres. Alonso's brilliance combined with the R25's consistency made him the youngest F1 champion in history at the time, aged just 24, breaking Michael Schumacher's fiv"},{"t":"IndyCar","k":"Page","p":"indycar.html","h":"","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"IndyCar is North America's premier single-seater series and the only championship that races on ovals, street circuits and road courses in a single season.…"},{"t":"A.J. Foyt","k":"Driver","p":"indycar.html","h":"driver-a-j-foyt","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"Super Tex · The toughest man ever to sit in an Indy car. A.J. Foyt won everything he entered: seven national championships, four Indianapolis 500s, the Daytona 500 and, with Dan Gurney, the 24 Hours of Le Mans. His 67 Indy car wins are still the all-time record, and he started a record 35 consecutive Indy 500s."},{"t":"Mario Andretti","k":"Driver","p":"indycar.html","h":"driver-mario-andretti","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"The Complete Racer · Born in Italy and raised in Pennsylvania, Mario Andretti became the most versatile driver in history. He is the only driver to win the Indianapolis 500, the Daytona 500 and the Formula 1 World Championship. His name became shorthand for speed in America, and his Indy car titles span almost two deca"},{"t":"Scott Dixon","k":"Driver","p":"indycar.html","h":"driver-scott-dixon","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"The Iceman · The greatest driver of the modern IndyCar era. Scott Dixon has driven for Chip Ganassi Racing since 2002, winning six championships across three different decades. His fuel-saving skill is legendary: he regularly wins races by going further on a tank than anyone thought possible. Only A.J. Foyt has more ti"},{"t":"Álex Palou","k":"Driver","p":"indycar.html","h":"driver-alex-palou","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"Modern Dominator · IndyCar's modern benchmark. Álex Palou joined Chip Ganassi Racing in 2021 and won the title in his first season with the team. He then won four championships in a row from 2023 to 2026. His 2025 season was one of the most dominant in series history, and his Indianapolis 500 win made him the first Spa"},{"t":"The Brickyard Opens & The First Indy 500","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-the-brickyard-opens-the-first-indy-500","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"1909–1911 · Carl Fisher builds the Indianapolis Motor Speedway in 1909 and resurfaces it with 3.2 million paving bricks, which gives the track its nickname. On 30 May 1911 Ray Harroun wins the first Indianapolis 500 in the Marmon Wasp, driving solo with a rear-view mirror instead of a riding mechanic."},{"t":"USAC Takes Control","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-usac-takes-control","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"1956 · After AAA withdraws from motorsport, the United States Auto Club (USAC) takes over the American national championship. The front-engine roadster era follows, with legends like A.J. Foyt and Parnelli Jones."},{"t":"The Rear-Engine Revolution","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-the-rear-engine-revolution","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"1965 · Jim Clark wins the Indy 500 in the rear-engined Lotus 38, the first rear-engined car to win the race. European F1 technology takes over and the front-engine roadster disappears almost overnight."},{"t":"CART Is Born","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-cart-is-born","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"1979 · Team owners led by Roger Penske, Pat Patrick and Dan Gurney break away from USAC to form Championship Auto Racing Teams (CART). Through the 1980s and early 90s CART becomes a global rival to F1, attracting stars like Nigel Mansell, who wins the 1993 title as the reigning F1 World Champion."},{"t":"The Split","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-the-split","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"1996–2007 · Speedway owner Tony George founds the rival Indy Racing League (IRL), built around the Indy 500. American open-wheel racing is divided into two series (CART, later Champ Car, and the IRL). The split drains sponsors, fans and TV audiences for more than a decade, while NASCAR's popularity booms."},{"t":"Reunification","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-reunification","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"2008 · Champ Car is absorbed into the IRL and the two series finally become one again, later branded the IndyCar Series. For the first time in twelve years, every top American open-wheel team races for the same championship."},{"t":"The Dallara DW12","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-the-dallara-dw12","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"2012 · A new spec chassis debuts, named in honour of Dan Wheldon, the two-time Indy 500 winner who tested it before losing his life at Las Vegas in 2011. Chevrolet and Honda supply turbocharged V6 engines, a rivalry that continues today."},{"t":"The Penske Era, Aeroscreen & Hybrid Power","k":"Timeline","p":"indycar.html","h":"event-the-penske-era-aeroscreen-hybrid-power","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"2020–2024 · Roger Penske buys IndyCar and the Indianapolis Motor Speedway. The Aeroscreen cockpit protection is introduced in 2020, and in July 2024 hybrid energy recovery arrives, adding a driver-deployed electric boost to the V6 engines."},{"t":"The First Indianapolis 500","k":"Race","p":"indycar.html","h":"story-the-first-indianapolis-500","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"30 May 1911 · Forty cars take the start of the International 500-Mile Sweepstakes on the brand-new brick surface. Ray Harroun's Marmon Wasp is the only single-seater in the field. Instead of carrying a riding mechanic to watch for traffic, Harroun fits a mirror, one of the first used on a car. He averages 74.6 mph and "},{"t":"Unser Jr. vs Goodyear","k":"Race","p":"indycar.html","h":"story-unser-jr-vs-goodyear","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"1992 Indy 500 · In one of the coldest Indy 500s ever, crashes eliminate many of the favourites. Al Unser Jr. holds off Scott Goodyear, who started last, by just 0.043 seconds, which stood as the closest finish in Indianapolis 500 history for 34 years. An emotional Unser Jr. tells the crowd: \"You just don't know what In"},{"t":"Hornish Snatches It at the Line","k":"Race","p":"indycar.html","h":"story-hornish-snatches-it-at-the-line","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"2006 Indy 500 · 19-year-old rookie Marco Andretti, grandson of Mario, looks certain to win after passing his own father Michael in the closing laps. On the final straight Sam Hornish Jr. slingshots out of his slipstream and wins by 0.0635 seconds. It is the first last-lap pass for the win in Indy 500 history, and the f"},{"t":"Wheldon's Final Victory","k":"Race","p":"indycar.html","h":"story-wheldon-s-final-victory","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"2011 Indy 500 · At the 100th anniversary race, rookie J.R. Hildebrand leads into the very last corner and then hits the wall while passing a slower car. Dan Wheldon, driving a part-time entry, steers around the wreck to win the Indy 500 for the second time. It is one of the most dramatic finishes the Speedway has ever "},{"t":"Rosenqvist by a Nose","k":"Race","p":"indycar.html","h":"story-rosenqvist-by-a-nose","s":"IndyCar","c":"var(--accent-indy)","x":"2026 Indy 500 · The 110th Indianapolis 500 set a record of 70 lead changes, 26 of them between Álex Palou and Scott Dixon alone. In the end, Felix Rosenqvist of Meyer Shank Racing beat David Malukas to the line by 0.0233 seconds to win his first Indy 500. It is the closest finish in the race's history, beating the 1992"},{"t":"WEC & Le Mans","k":"Page","p":"wec.html","h":"","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"The FIA World Endurance Championship represents the pinnacle of international sports car racing, with the 24 Hours of Le Mans as its jewel. Since its modern…"},{"t":"Tom Kristensen","k":"Driver","p":"wec.html","h":"driver-tom-kristensen","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"Mr. Le Mans · Nobody has won Le Mans more often. Tom Kristensen won the 24 Hours nine times, including six in a row from 2000 to 2005, mostly with Audi but also with Bentley in 2003. He was brilliant at night, in traffic and in the rain, the qualities that win endurance races. He retired in 2014 as the 2013 World Endur"},{"t":"Jacky Ickx","k":"Driver","p":"wec.html","h":"driver-jacky-ickx","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"The Rain Master · Jacky Ickx changed Le Mans forever. In 1969 he protested against the dangerous running start by walking calmly to his car and buckling his belts, starting last. He then won the race, and from 1970 drivers started already strapped in. He went on to win Le Mans six times and finished as F1 runner-up twi"},{"t":"Sébastien Buemi","k":"Driver","p":"wec.html","h":"driver-sebastien-buemi","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"Toyota Loyalist · The most successful driver of the modern WEC. After three F1 seasons with Toro Rosso, Sébastien Buemi joined Toyota in 2012 and has stayed with the team ever since. He was part of Toyota's long-awaited first Le Mans win in 2018 and won three more after it. He also won the Formula E title."},{"t":"917K","k":"Car","p":"wec.html","h":"car-917k","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"Porsche · The car that gave Porsche its first Le Mans win. The flat-12 917 was terrifying at first, until aerodynamic work created the short-tail 917K. It won Le Mans in 1970 and 1971, and the 1971 winner set a distance record that stood until 2010. The Gulf and Salzburg liveries remain icons."},{"t":"R10 TDI","k":"Car","p":"wec.html","h":"car-r10-tdi","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"Audi Sport · The first diesel to win Le Mans. Only 200 days after it was unveiled, the twin-turbo V12 R10 won Le Mans in 2006, then won again in 2007 and 2008. Its huge torque and fuel efficiency let it run longer between stops than its rivals. It won 36 of the 48 races it entered."},{"t":"919 Hybrid","k":"Car","p":"wec.html","h":"car-919-hybrid","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"Porsche · The peak of the LMP1 hybrid era. A tiny 2.0-litre V4 turbo combined with two energy recovery systems made around 900hp. The 919 won Le Mans and the WEC title three years in a row from 2015 to 2017. In 2018 the unrestricted 919 Evo lapped the Nürburgring Nordschleife in 5:19.546."},{"t":"The Original World Championship","k":"History","p":"wec.html","h":"story-the-original-world-championship","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"1953–1992 · The World Sportscar Championship ran under various names from 1953, with legendary battles between Ferrari, Jaguar, Mercedes-Benz, Maserati, Porsche and Aston Martin. Its golden years included the Porsche 917 versus Ferrari 512 fights of 1970–71 and the Group C era of the 1980s. Rising costs from the 3.5-li"},{"t":"The Le Mans Series Era","k":"History","p":"wec.html","h":"story-the-le-mans-series-era","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"2004–2011 · The Le Mans Series keeps endurance racing alive in Europe while the American Le Mans Series carries the flag in the USA. Audi dominates with the R8 and then the R10 TDI, which in 2006 becomes the first diesel car to win Le Mans. Peugeot strikes back with the 908 HDi FAP, starting a diesel war that wins Peug"},{"t":"The Modern WEC & Hypercar Era","k":"History","p":"wec.html","h":"story-the-modern-wec-hypercar-era","s":"WEC & Le Mans","c":"var(--accent-wec)","x":"2012–Present · The FIA World Endurance Championship is reborn in 2012 for the LMP1 hybrid era. Audi, Toyota and Porsche field extremely complex hybrid prototypes with around 1,000bhp combined. In 2017 Kamui Kobayashi's Toyota TS050 sets the fastest lap ever on the current Le Mans layout (3:14.791), and in 2018 an unres"},{"t":"GT3","k":"Page","p":"gt3.html","h":"","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"GT3 represents the golden balance between accessibility and performance. Introduced in 2006, the GT3 class allows wealthy privateers and gentleman drivers…"},{"t":"Laurens Vanthoor","k":"Driver","p":"gt3.html","h":"driver-laurens-vanthoor","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"Porsche Ace · One of the finest GT drivers of his generation. Laurens Vanthoor first made his name with Audi, winning the Spa 24 Hours and the Nürburgring 24 Hours before becoming a Porsche factory driver. He has since won GT titles on both sides of the Atlantic, and in 2024 he became the WEC Hypercar World Champion wi"},{"t":"Augusto Farfus","k":"Driver","p":"gt3.html","h":"driver-augusto-farfus","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"BMW Legend · BMW's most loyal ace. Augusto Farfus has spent most of his career as a BMW factory driver, in touring cars, DTM and GT racing. He won the Nürburgring 24 Hours overall in 2010 and again in 2025, fifteen years apart. Known for his aggressive, committed style, he also won the FIA GT World Cup in Macau."},{"t":"Christopher Mies","k":"Driver","p":"gt3.html","h":"driver-christopher-mies","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"Audi Master · Audi's GT3 specialist. Christopher Mies spent fifteen years as an Audi factory driver, winning the Nürburgring 24 Hours and the Bathurst 12 Hour with the R8 LMS. His feedback helped make the R8 LMS one of GT3's most successful cars. Mies is the model modern GT3 driver: fast, consistent and technically bri"},{"t":"911 GT3 R","k":"Car","p":"gt3.html","h":"car-911-gt3-r","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"Porsche Motorsport · One of the most successful GT3 cars in history. Porsche's 911 GT3 R has won countless championships worldwide thanks to its balance, traction from its rear-mounted flat-six, and unmatched reliability. Drivers like Laurens Vanthoor have built entire careers dominating in the 911 GT3 R across IMSA, W"},{"t":"Z4 GT3","k":"Car","p":"gt3.html","h":"car-z4-gt3","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"BMW Motorsport · An instant classic. The BMW Z4 GT3 won the Dubai 24 Hour in its debut season, took two Super GT GT300 titles and won the 2015 Spa 24 Hours with Marc VDS in its final year. Its screaming naturally aspirated V8, taken from the M3 GT2, made it one of GT3's most iconic sounds."},{"t":"R8 LMS","k":"Car","p":"gt3.html","h":"car-r8-lms","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"Audi Sport · Audi's GT3 weapon. The R8 LMS brought endurance racing pedigree to GT3, with victories at the Nurburgring and Spa 24 Hours becoming routine. Drivers like Christopher Mies and René Rast have extracted everything from the R8's high-revving V10, an engine shared with the road car. The R8 LMS proves Audi's mot"},{"t":"The Philosophy of GT3","k":"History","p":"gt3.html","h":"story-the-philosophy-of-gt3","s":"GT3","c":"var(--accent-gt3)","x":"2006 - Present · GT3 was designed as the \"customer racing\" class: cars that wealthy amateurs and professional drivers could purchase from manufacturers and race competitively. Unlike GT1 or LMP1, GT3 focuses on close racing through Balance of Performance (BoP) regulations that adjust each car's weight, power, and aerod"},{"t":"GT1 Era","k":"Page","p":"gt1.html","h":"","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"From 1994 to 1999, the BPR Global GT Series and then the FIA GT Championship produced the most spectacular and emotionally charged GT racing ever witnessed.…"},{"t":"F1 GTR","k":"Car","p":"gt1.html","h":"car-f1-gtr","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"McLaren · A road car that won Le Mans. Gordon Murray's McLaren F1 was never designed to race, but the GTR version with its BMW V12 won Le Mans in 1995 on its first attempt. In 1997 the 'Longtail' version arrived to fight Mercedes and Porsche. 28 racing chassis were built."},{"t":"CLK GTR","k":"Car","p":"gt1.html","h":"car-clk-gtr","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"AMG-Mercedes · Built to win from a blank sheet of paper. The CLK GTR was developed as a racing car first, with road cars built afterwards to satisfy homologation rules. It won the FIA GT title in 1997, and with its CLK LM successor Mercedes won every race in 1998."},{"t":"911 GT1","k":"Car","p":"gt1.html","h":"car-911-gt1","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"Porsche · A 911 in name only. The GT1 put a water-cooled twin-turbo flat-six in the middle of the car and wore 911-style headlights. It finished 2nd at Le Mans on its 1996 debut, and in 1998 the GT1-98 gave Porsche a 1-2 finish and its 16th Le Mans win."},{"t":"BPR Global GT Series Launched","k":"Timeline","p":"gt1.html","h":"event-bpr-global-gt-series-launched","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"1994 · Stéphane Ratel, Jürgen Barth and Patrick Peter create the BPR series, a pan-European championship for genuine GT supercars. The Ferrari F40, Porsche 911 GT2 and Venturi 600LM headline the grids, and the series becomes one of the most spectacular in the world."},{"t":"McLaren F1 GTR Conquers Le Mans","k":"Timeline","p":"gt1.html","h":"event-mclaren-f1-gtr-conquers-le-mans","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"1995 · The McLaren F1 GTR, a racing version of a road car, dominates the BPR series and then wins Le Mans outright against purpose-built prototypes. JJ Lehto, Yannick Dalmas and Masanori Sekiya win a race with around 17 hours of rain, and McLarens finish 1st, 3rd, 4th and 5th."},{"t":"Porsche 911 GT1 & The Rules Are Stretched","k":"Timeline","p":"gt1.html","h":"event-porsche-911-gt1-the-rules-are-stretched","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"1996 · Porsche arrives with the 911 GT1, a purpose-built racer with only a few road versions made to satisfy homologation. It finishes 2nd overall at Le Mans on its debut. From now on, GT1 cars are race cars first and road cars second."},{"t":"FIA GT Championship & The Mercedes CLK GTR","k":"Timeline","p":"gt1.html","h":"event-fia-gt-championship-the-mercedes-clk-gtr","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"1997 · The FIA takes over the BPR series and renames it the FIA GT Championship. Mercedes-Benz arrives with the V12 CLK GTR, and McLaren answers with the F1 GTR \"Longtail\". The two battle all year, and Bernd Schneider wins the title for Mercedes."},{"t":"Total Mercedes Domination","k":"Timeline","p":"gt1.html","h":"event-total-mercedes-domination","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"1998 · Mercedes wins all ten FIA GT rounds with the CLK GTR and the new CLK LM, and Klaus Ludwig and Ricardo Zonta take the title. At Le Mans, though, the Porsche 911 GT1-98 finishes 1st and 2nd overall."},{"t":"The End of GT1","k":"Timeline","p":"gt1.html","h":"event-the-end-of-gt1","s":"GT1 Era","c":"var(--accent-gt1)","x":"1999 · With too few entries, the FIA drops the GT1 class and the Chrysler Viper GTS-R becomes the new top GT car. At Le Mans, Mercedes' CLR prototypes take off at speed, twice with Mark Webber and once with Peter Dumbreck in the race. Mercedes withdraws, and the wildest era of GT racing is over."},{"t":"NASCAR","k":"Page","p":"nascar.html","h":"","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"Born on the beaches of Daytona, NASCAR is America's most-watched form of motorsport. Forty heavy, powerful stock cars race inches apart on banked ovals,…"},{"t":"Richard Petty","k":"Driver","p":"nascar.html","h":"driver-richard-petty","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"The King · Nobody will ever catch \"The King\". Richard Petty won 200 Cup races, almost twice as many as anyone else, and a record seven Daytona 500s. In 1967 alone he won 27 races, including ten in a row. With his No. 43, cowboy hat and sunglasses, he became the face of NASCAR for generations."},{"t":"Dale Earnhardt","k":"Driver","p":"nascar.html","h":"driver-dale-earnhardt","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"The Intimidator · The most feared driver NASCAR has ever seen. In his black No. 3 Chevrolet for Richard Childress Racing, Dale Earnhardt won seven titles with an aggressive style that earned him the nickname \"The Intimidator\". He finally won the Daytona 500 in 1998 on his 20th attempt. His death on the last lap of the "},{"t":"Jimmie Johnson","k":"Driver","p":"nascar.html","h":"driver-jimmie-johnson","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"Five-Time · The most dominant driver of the modern era. With Hendrick Motorsports and crew chief Chad Knaus, Jimmie Johnson won five championships in a row from 2006 to 2010, something no one else has done, and added two more to tie Petty and Earnhardt's record. He later raced in IndyCar and at Le Mans."},{"t":"Superbird","k":"Car","p":"nascar.html","h":"car-superbird","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"Plymouth · The car that brought Richard Petty back to Plymouth. With its pointed nose cone and huge rear wing, the Superbird was built for one thing: speed on NASCAR's superspeedways. Petty won 18 races in 1970, many of them in the Superbird. It was so effective that NASCAR restricted the winged cars from 1971."},{"t":"Thunderbird (1987)","k":"Car","p":"nascar.html","h":"car-thunderbird-1987","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"Ford · The fastest car NASCAR has ever seen. \"Awesome Bill from Dawsonville\" qualified his Thunderbird at Talladega at 212.809 mph in 1987, with an engine built by his brother Ernie. Restrictor plates arrived the next year, so the record will probably never be broken."},{"t":"Next Gen","k":"Car","p":"nascar.html","h":"car-next-gen","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"Chevrolet · Ford · Toyota · NASCAR's modern race car. The Next Gen brought a five-speed sequential gearbox, independent rear suspension and 18-inch wheels with a single centre lug nut, much closer to sports car technology. Chevrolet Camaro, Ford Mustang and Toyota Camry bodies share many standard parts to keep racing c"},{"t":"NASCAR Is Born","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-nascar-is-born","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"1948–1949 · Bill France Sr. founds the National Association for Stock Car Auto Racing in Daytona Beach. In 1949 the first \"Strictly Stock\" race runs at Charlotte, the start of what is now the NASCAR Cup Series."},{"t":"Daytona & The First Daytona 500","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-daytona-the-first-daytona-500","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"1959 · Daytona International Speedway opens with its high-banked 2.5-mile tri-oval. The first Daytona 500 ends in a photo finish, and Lee Petty, Richard Petty's father, is declared the winner days later."},{"t":"The Winged Warriors","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-the-winged-warriors","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"1970 · Dodge's Charger Daytona and Plymouth's Superbird bring nose cones and towering rear wings to stock car racing. Buddy Baker becomes the first driver to lap a closed course at over 200 mph. NASCAR restricts the winged cars from 1971."},{"t":"The Fight That Made NASCAR","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-the-fight-that-made-nascar","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"1979 · The Daytona 500 is shown live from start to finish on national TV for the first time. A last-lap crash between leaders Cale Yarborough and Donnie Allison turns into a fistfight on the infield, Richard Petty wins, and millions of new fans tune in."},{"t":"Too Fast: The Restrictor Plate Era","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-too-fast-the-restrictor-plate-era","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"1987–1988 · Bill Elliott qualifies at Talladega at 212.809 mph, still the fastest in NASCAR history. Days later, in the race itself, Bobby Allison's car rips into the catch fence. From 1988 NASCAR forces restrictor plates on cars at Daytona and Talladega to keep speeds down."},{"t":"Losing Dale Earnhardt","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-losing-dale-earnhardt","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"2001 · Seven-time champion Dale Earnhardt dies in a last-lap crash at the Daytona 500. NASCAR makes the HANS head-and-neck restraint mandatory that year and starts installing SAFER energy-absorbing barriers, changes that have saved countless drivers since."},{"t":"The Playoffs Arrive","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-the-playoffs-arrive","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"2004 · NASCAR introduces \"The Chase\", a playoff format that decides the champion over the final races of the season. Jimmie Johnson masters it, winning five titles in a row from 2006 to 2010."},{"t":"The Next Gen Car","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-the-next-gen-car","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"2022 · The seventh-generation Cup car debuts at the Daytona 500. It brings a sequential gearbox, independent rear suspension and 18-inch single-lug wheels, the biggest technical change in NASCAR's history."},{"t":"The Chase Returns","k":"Timeline","p":"nascar.html","h":"event-the-chase-returns","s":"NASCAR","c":"var(--accent-nascar)","x":"2026 · NASCAR drops the knockout playoff format it had used since 2014 and brings back the Chase. The top 16 drivers in the points battle it out over the final 10 races with no eliminations, and a race win is now worth 55 points."},{"t":"Super GT","k":"Page","p":"super-gt.html","h":"","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"Super GT is Japan's biggest motor race series. GT500 cars from Toyota, Nissan and Honda share the track with a huge GT300 field, and success ballast keeps…"},{"t":"Ronnie Quintarelli","k":"Driver","p":"super-gt.html","h":"driver-ronnie-quintarelli","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"Nissan Legend · An Italian who became a superstar in Japan. Ronnie Quintarelli moved to Japanese racing early in his career and became Nissan's GT500 spearhead. He won back-to-back titles with Masataka Yanagida in 2011 and 2012, then two more with Tsugio Matsuda in 2014 and 2015, all in the GT-R."},{"t":"Sho Tsuboi","k":"Driver","p":"super-gt.html","h":"driver-sho-tsuboi","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"Modern Dominator · The man of the moment in Super GT. Sho Tsuboi won the GT500 title with TOM'S Toyota in 2021, 2023, 2024 and 2025, and helped TOM'S become the first team to win three in a row. He added the 2024 Super Formula title, making him one of the best all-round drivers in Japan today."},{"t":"Satoshi Motoyama","k":"Driver","p":"super-gt.html","h":"driver-satoshi-motoyama","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"Mr. Nissan · Japan's racing hero of the 2000s. Satoshi Motoyama spent almost his entire GT500 career with Nissan and won three titles, including the GT-R's first season in 2008. He also won four Formula Nippon single-seater championships and tested an F1 car for Jordan and Renault."},{"t":"GT-R GT500","k":"Car","p":"super-gt.html","h":"car-gt-r-gt500","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"NISMO · The most successful car in GT500 history. The R35 GT-R won seven of its first nine races in 2008 and claimed five drivers' titles. Its 41 wins are a GT500 record. From 2014 it used a 2.0-litre turbo four-cylinder producing about 640hp."},{"t":"GR Supra GT500","k":"Car","p":"super-gt.html","h":"car-gr-supra-gt500","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"Toyota Gazoo Racing · The car of the modern era. The GR Supra replaced the Lexus LC in 2020, bringing the famous Supra name back to GT500. In the hands of TOM'S and Sho Tsuboi it won the title in 2021 and then three years in a row from 2023 to 2025."},{"t":"NSX-GT","k":"Car","p":"super-gt.html","h":"car-nsx-gt","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"Honda · Honda's mid-engined GT500 weapon. The NSX-GT carried Jenson Button and Naoki Yamamoto to the 2018 title, and Yamamoto won again in 2020. After 2023 it was replaced by the Civic Type R-GT."},{"t":"The JGTC Is Born","k":"Timeline","p":"super-gt.html","h":"event-the-jgtc-is-born","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"1993 · The All Japan Grand Touring Car Championship starts with two classes racing together, today called GT500 and GT300. Nissan's Skyline GT-R, Toyota's Supra and later Honda's NSX begin a manufacturer rivalry that still drives the series."},{"t":"Honda's First Title","k":"Timeline","p":"super-gt.html","h":"event-honda-s-first-title","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"2000 · Ryo Michigami wins the GT500 title in the Mugen NSX, Honda's first championship in the series, without winning a single race. Four second places are enough."},{"t":"Super GT","k":"Timeline","p":"super-gt.html","h":"event-super-gt","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"2005 · The JGTC is renamed Super GT as it grows its international ambitions. Fans love the format: two classes, close racing, and success ballast that adds weight to the cars that score the most points."},{"t":"The GT-R Arrives","k":"Timeline","p":"super-gt.html","h":"event-the-gt-r-arrives","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"2008 · Nissan's new R35 GT-R wins seven of its first nine races and takes the title with Satoshi Motoyama and Benoît Tréluyer. It goes on to win 41 GT500 races, more than any other model."},{"t":"Turbo Four-Cylinders & Shared Rules","k":"Timeline","p":"super-gt.html","h":"event-turbo-four-cylinders-shared-rules","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"2014 · GT500 switches to 2.0-litre turbocharged four-cylinder engines and rules shared with Germany's DTM. The rules became fully identical as \"Class One\" in 2020, and in 2019 the two series raced each other in a Dream Race at Fuji."},{"t":"An F1 Champion Wins in Japan","k":"Timeline","p":"super-gt.html","h":"event-an-f1-champion-wins-in-japan","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"2018 · 2009 Formula 1 World Champion Jenson Button wins the GT500 title alongside Naoki Yamamoto in the Honda NSX-GT."},{"t":"TOM'S Makes History","k":"Timeline","p":"super-gt.html","h":"event-tom-s-makes-history","s":"Super GT","c":"var(--accent-supergt)","x":"2023–2025 · Toyota's TOM'S team becomes the first in GT500 history to win three championships in a row. Sho Tsuboi's 2025 title is his fourth, equalling Ronnie Quintarelli's record."},{"t":"DTM","k":"Page","p":"dtm.html","h":"","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"The DTM is Germany's home of touring car and GT racing. From the boxy homologation specials of the 1980s to today's GT3 cars, it has always been a…"},{"t":"Bernd Schneider","k":"Driver","p":"dtm.html","h":"driver-bernd-schneider","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"Mr. DTM · The most successful DTM driver ever. Bernd Schneider won the 1995 title in the original DTM, then four more after its revival, all with Mercedes. His 43 wins are still the series record, and in 1997 he also won the FIA GT Championship in the CLK GTR."},{"t":"Klaus Ludwig","k":"Driver","p":"dtm.html","h":"driver-klaus-ludwig","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"König Ludwig · \"King Ludwig\" won almost everything in German motorsport. He took DTM titles with Ford and twice with Mercedes, won Le Mans three times with Porsche, and was crowned FIA GT champion in 1998. He came back to the DTM in 2000 at the age of 50."},{"t":"René Rast","k":"Driver","p":"dtm.html","h":"driver-rene-rast","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"Title Machine · The best of the modern era. René Rast won the DTM title in 2017, his first full season, in an Audi RS 5. He won twice more in 2019 and 2020 in the Class One turbo era, and retired from the series in 2025 with 31 wins, third on the all-time list."},{"t":"190E Evo II","k":"Car","p":"dtm.html","h":"car-190e-evo-ii","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"AMG-Mercedes · The winged legend. Mercedes built 502 road cars of the Evolution II so it could race, complete with its giant rear wing. In 1992 the Evo II won 16 of 24 DTM races and Mercedes drivers finished 1-2-3, with Klaus Ludwig the champion."},{"t":"155 V6 TI","k":"Car","p":"dtm.html","h":"car-155-v6-ti","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"Alfa Corse · The Italian invader. A high-revving V6 and four-wheel drive made the 155 V6 TI almost unbeatable in its debut season. Nicola Larini won 11 of 22 races in 1993 to claim the title, beating Mercedes on home ground."},{"t":"RS 5 DTM","k":"Car","p":"dtm.html","h":"car-rs-5-dtm","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"Audi Sport · Audi's championship machine of the 2010s. Mike Rockenfeller won the title in its debut season in 2013. René Rast won in 2017 with the V8 version, then twice more in 2019 and 2020 after the switch to a 2.0-litre turbo four-cylinder under Class One rules."},{"t":"From Production Cars to DTM","k":"Timeline","p":"dtm.html","h":"event-from-production-cars-to-dtm","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"1984–1986 · Germany's touring car championship starts in 1984 as the Deutsche Produktionswagen-Meisterschaft and is renamed DTM in 1986. Factory-backed BMW M3s, Mercedes 190Es and Ford Sierras soon battle in front of huge crowds."},{"t":"The Homologation Specials","k":"Timeline","p":"dtm.html","h":"event-the-homologation-specials","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"1988–1992 · Manufacturers build road-going \"evolution\" models to race. Klaus Ludwig wins the 1988 title in a Ford Sierra RS500 and the 1992 title in the winged Mercedes 190E Evo II, as Mercedes takes a 1-2-3 in the standings."},{"t":"Class 1 & The Alfa Invasion","k":"Timeline","p":"dtm.html","h":"event-class-1-the-alfa-invasion","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"1993 · New Class 1 rules allow far more freedom. Alfa Romeo arrives with the four-wheel-drive 155 V6 TI and Nicola Larini wins the title in its first season, winning 11 of 22 races."},{"t":"ITC & Collapse","k":"Timeline","p":"dtm.html","h":"event-itc-collapse","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"1995–1996 · The series goes international as the ITC, but the technology and costs explode. Alfa Romeo and Opel pull out after 1996 and the championship collapses."},{"t":"Reborn as the Masters","k":"Timeline","p":"dtm.html","h":"event-reborn-as-the-masters","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"2000 · The DTM returns as the Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters with V8 silhouette racers from Mercedes, Opel and Audi. Bernd Schneider wins the first two titles of the new era."},{"t":"Class One Turbo Era","k":"Timeline","p":"dtm.html","h":"event-class-one-turbo-era","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"2019 · DTM switches to 2.0-litre turbocharged four-cylinder engines under \"Class One\" rules shared with Japan's Super GT. René Rast wins back-to-back titles in 2019 and 2020 for Audi."},{"t":"GT3 & A New Owner","k":"Timeline","p":"dtm.html","h":"event-gt3-a-new-owner","s":"DTM","c":"var(--accent-dtm)","x":"2021–2023 · With manufacturers leaving, DTM switches to GT3 cars in 2021, opening the grid to Mercedes-AMG, BMW, Porsche, Ferrari, Lamborghini and more. In 2023 the German motoring club ADAC takes over the series."},{"t":"Legendary Circuits","k":"Page","p":"circuits.html","h":"","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"The tracks that define each championship on this site, from the streets of Monte Carlo to the banking of Daytona."},{"t":"Circuit de Monaco and Monte Carlo","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-circuit-de-monaco-and-monte-carlo","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"3.337 KM 19 CORNERS 78 LAPS · First raced in 1929, Monaco runs through the narrow streets of Monte Carlo, with barriers just centimetres from the cars. The track climbs 44 metres from the harbour to Casino Square, dives through a tunnel where cars lose up to a third of their downforce, and includes the Fairmont Hairpin"},{"t":"Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps in Belgium","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-circuit-de-spa-francorchamps-in-belgium","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"7.004 KM 19 CORNERS EAU ROUGE · Spa opened in 1921 as a 15 km loop of public roads through the Ardennes forest, and was shortened to today's 7 km layout in 1979. It is still the longest track on the F1 calendar. The weather is famous for being unpredictable: it can rain on one part of the circuit while another stays dr"},{"t":"Indianapolis Motor Speedway in the USA","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-indianapolis-motor-speedway-in-the-usa","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"4.023 KM 4 CORNERS 200 LAPS · Opened in 1909, the 2.5-mile rectangular oval has four corners banked at only 9 degrees, flat by American standards, which makes car setup critical. With more than 257,000 permanent seats it is the largest sports venue in the world, and the only racing circuit that is a US National Histori"},{"t":"Circuit de la Sarthe in Le Mans, France","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-circuit-de-la-sarthe-in-le-mans-france","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"13.626 KM 24 HOURS MULSANNE STRAIGHT · Up to 85% of the Circuit de la Sarthe is made of public roads that are closed only for the race. Its 6 km Mulsanne Straight once let cars pass 400 km/h: Roger Dorchy's WM Peugeot was timed at 407 km/h in 1988. Two chicanes were added in 1990 to slow the cars down. First raced in 1"},{"t":"Daytona International Speedway in Florida, USA","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-daytona-international-speedway-in-florida-usa","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"5.73 KM 12 CORNERS BANKED OVAL · Daytona opened in 1959 with the first Daytona 500. Its 2.5-mile tri-oval is banked at 31 degrees in the turns and 18 degrees at the start/finish line. The 24-hour road course links the infield with most of the banked oval, so sports cars run flat out on the banking before braking hard f"},{"t":"Suzuka Circuit in Japan","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-suzuka-circuit-in-japan","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"5.807 KM 18 TURNS FIGURE-EIGHT · Suzuka is the only Grade 1 circuit in the world laid out as a figure-eight, with the back straight crossing over the front section on a bridge. Opened by Honda in 1962, its S Curves, Degner, Spoon and the flat-out 130R make it a favourite of F1 and Super GT drivers, and many F1 titles h"},{"t":"Nürburgring Nordschleife in Germany","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-nurburgring-nordschleife-in-germany","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"20.832 KM 300 M+ ELEVATION GREEN HELL · Jackie Stewart named it the \"Green Hell\" after winning there in rain and fog in 1968. Opened in 1927, the 20.8 km loop through the Eifel forest was dropped from F1 after Niki Lauda's fiery crash in 1976, but it still hosts the Nürburgring 24 Hours. The fastest lap ever, 5:19.546,"},{"t":"Sebring International Raceway in Florida, USA","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-sebring-international-raceway-in-florida-usa","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"6.019 KM 17 TURNS 12 HOURS · Sebring was built on a World War II airfield, and parts of the track still use the old concrete runways. Their bumps and seams send sparks flying from the cars and make the 12 Hours of Sebring, first held in 1952, one of the hardest tests in endurance racing. It also hosted the first United"},{"t":"Mount Panorama in Bathurst, Australia","k":"Circuit","p":"circuits.html","h":"circuit-mount-panorama-in-bathurst-australia","s":"Circuits","c":"var(--gold)","x":"6.213 KM 23 TURNS 174 M CLIMB · When it isn't hosting races, Mount Panorama is a public road with a 60 km/h speed limit and houses along it. The track climbs 174 metres up the mountain and plunges back down through the Esses to Conrod Straight. The official lap record, 1:59.291, belongs to GT3 legend Christopher Mies i"},{"t":"Compare the Cars","k":"Page","p":"compare.html","h":"","s":"Compare","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Every car on this site side by side. Filter by championship, or click a column heading to sort by power, weight or year."},{"t":"How a Race Weekend Works","k":"Page","p":"race-weekend.html","h":"","s":"Race Weekend","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Every championship follows the same basic rhythm: practise, qualify, race. The details are very different, though, and they change how each series is won."},{"t":"Time Penalty","k":"Term","p":"race-weekend.html","h":"term-time-penalty","s":"Race Weekend","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Most common · Usually 5 or 10 seconds added to a driver's time, or served at their next pit stop before the crew can touch the car. Given for things like causing a collision or repeatedly breaking track limits."},{"t":"Drive-Through / Stop-and-Go","k":"Term","p":"race-weekend.html","h":"term-drive-through-stop-and-go","s":"Race Weekend","c":"var(--gold)","x":"More serious · The driver must drive through the pit lane at the speed limit, or stop in their pit box for a set time, losing a lot of ground. Used for bigger offences, like speeding in the pit lane or unsafe driving."},{"t":"Grid Penalty","k":"Term","p":"race-weekend.html","h":"term-grid-penalty","s":"Race Weekend","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Before the race · The driver starts further back than they qualified. In F1, the most common reason is using more engine or gearbox parts than the rules allow for the season."},{"t":"Pass Through / Back of the Field","k":"Term","p":"race-weekend.html","h":"term-pass-through-back-of-the-field","s":"Race Weekend","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Ovals & NASCAR · On ovals, penalties are often served by driving through the pits under green, or by being sent to the back of the field at the next restart under caution."},{"t":"Racing Terms","k":"Page","p":"terms.html","h":"","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Every sport has its own language. Here's a plain-English guide to every term used throughout this website so nothing goes over your head."},{"t":"IMSA","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-imsa","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · Stands for International Motor Sports Association . It is the governing body and championship series for sports car racing in North America. Think of it as the organisation that organises races like the Daytona 24h and sets the rules all teams must follow."},{"t":"Formula 1 (F1)","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-formula-1-f1","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · The FIA Formula 1 World Championship is the pinnacle of single-seater racing and the most prestigious motorsport series in the world. F1 cars are purpose-built, open-wheel racing machines representing the absolute cutting edge of automotive technology. Championships run from March to December w"},{"t":"IndyCar","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-indycar","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · The NTT IndyCar Series is North America's top single-seater championship. All teams use the same Dallara chassis with either a Chevrolet or Honda engine, so driver skill and team strategy decide races. It is the only major series that races on ovals, street circuits and road courses in the same"},{"t":"FIA WEC","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-fia-wec","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · The FIA World Endurance Championship — the global version of IMSA. Run by the FIA (the worldwide governing body of motorsport), WEC races across multiple continents with Le Mans as its centrepiece event. \"World\" means it visits several continents, with rounds in Europe, Asia and the Americas."},{"t":"BPR Global GT Series","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-bpr-global-gt-series","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · A privately-organised racing series founded in 1994 by Barth, Peter and Ratel (the B, P and R). It gave GT supercars a proper international championship to race in before the FIA took over and renamed it the FIA GT Championship in 1997."},{"t":"Grand Prix","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-grand-prix","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · French for \"Grand Prize\" — the name given to each individual F1 race. Each Grand Prix is named after its host country or region (e.g., Monaco Grand Prix, British Grand Prix). A full F1 season consists of approximately 20–24 Grands Prix held worldwide."},{"t":"Pole Position","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-pole-position","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · The first starting position on the grid, awarded to the driver who sets the fastest qualifying lap time. Starting from pole gives a significant advantage as the driver leads into the first corner with clear track ahead. Lewis Hamilton holds the all-time record with more than 100 pole positions."},{"t":"DRS (Drag Reduction System)","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-drs-drag-reduction-system","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · A moveable rear wing flap that reduced drag to give extra speed on straights. From 2011 to 2025, drivers could open it in set zones when within one second of the car ahead, to help overtaking. For 2026 it was replaced by active aerodynamics on every car and an electric \"overtake mode\" boost."},{"t":"Qualifying","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-qualifying","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · A time-trial session held before the race to determine the starting grid order. Qualifying is split into three knockout rounds (Q1, Q2, Q3), with the slowest drivers eliminated after each round. The fastest driver in Q3 earns pole position."},{"t":"Constructor","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-constructor","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · The team/manufacturer that builds the F1 car — examples include Mercedes, Ferrari, Red Bull Racing, McLaren. The Constructors' Championship is awarded to the team that scores the most combined points from both of its drivers across a season. It's considered just as prestigious as the Drivers' Championship."},{"t":"Parc Ferme","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-parc-ferme","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · French for \"closed park\" — a strict set of regulations that prevents teams from making significant changes to their cars between qualifying and the race. Once a car enters parc fermé (after qualifying), only minor adjustments are allowed. This ensures teams don't run special qualifying setups."},{"t":"Power Unit","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-power-unit","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · Modern F1's term for the complete propulsion system — a 1.6-litre turbocharged V6 combined with electric motor-generators that harvest and deploy energy. Since 2026 the MGU-H has been dropped and the electric share has grown to roughly half of the total output, running on fully sustainable fuel. They are the"},{"t":"Undercut / Overcut","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-undercut-overcut","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"F1 Terms · Pit stop strategies used to gain track position. An undercut means pitting earlier than your rival to gain an advantage with fresh tyres on clear track. An overcut is the opposite — staying out longer on older tyres, hoping the extra track time gains more than fresh tyres would."},{"t":"Indianapolis 500","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-indianapolis-500","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · The 500-mile race (200 laps) held every May at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway since 1911. Known as \"The Greatest Spectacle in Racing\", it draws more than 300,000 spectators and is part of motorsport's Triple Crown. It is so important that many drivers value winning it more than the IndyCar championship"},{"t":"Oval Racing","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-oval-racing","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · Racing on a closed loop of banked left-hand turns , like Indianapolis or Texas. Speeds are extremely high (over 370 km/h at Indy) and cars run nose-to-tail in each other's slipstream for lap after lap. The skills are completely different from road courses: tiny setup changes and bravery in traffic make "},{"t":"Push-to-Pass","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-push-to-pass","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · A button that gives a temporary power boost (around 60 extra horsepower) on road and street courses. Each driver gets a fixed number of seconds to use across the whole race, so they must choose between attacking, defending or saving it for the final laps. It is IndyCar's answer to F1's DRS, but it can b"},{"t":"Aeroscreen","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-aeroscreen","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · A ballistic screen that wraps around the cockpit , introduced in 2020. It combines a titanium frame (similar to F1's Halo) with a toughened polycarbonate windscreen that protects the driver's head from debris. It is especially important on ovals, where parts can fly off at more than 350 km/h."},{"t":"Rolling Start","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-rolling-start","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · Unlike F1's standing starts, IndyCar races usually begin with the field already moving behind the pace car. When the pace car pulls into the pits, the leader controls the pace until the green flag waves. At the Indy 500 the 33 cars line up in eleven rows of three, a tradition unique to the race."},{"t":"Full-Course Caution","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-full-course-caution","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · When there is a crash or debris, IndyCar brings out the yellow flag for the whole track and sends out a pace car. The field bunches up behind it and the pit lane usually closes at first. Cautions reset the gaps between cars, which is why IndyCar races so often end with close finishes and dramatic strate"},{"t":"Bump Day","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-bump-day","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · The Indy 500 has just 33 starting spots . When more cars enter than that, the slowest qualifiers are \"bumped\" out of the race in a tense shootout now called Last Chance Qualifying. Even famous names have failed to make the field, including Fernando Alonso with McLaren in 2019."},{"t":"Drinking the Milk","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-drinking-the-milk","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · The Indy 500 winner celebrates by drinking a bottle of milk in Victory Lane instead of spraying champagne. It began when Louis Meyer drank buttermilk after winning in 1936, and it became an official tradition in 1956. Before the race, each driver even tells the organisers whether they would like whole, "},{"t":"Triple Crown of Motorsport","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-triple-crown-of-motorsport","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"IndyCar Terms · The unofficial title for winning the Monaco Grand Prix, the Indianapolis 500 and the 24 Hours of Le Mans during a career. Only Graham Hill has ever achieved it. Fernando Alonso has won Monaco and Le Mans and went to Indianapolis several times to try to complete the set."},{"t":"GT1 Class","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-gt1-class","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Car Classes · The top class of GT racing in the late 1990s. Cars had to be based on a road-going production vehicle but are heavily modified for racing. GT1 cars produced 600+ horsepower and were the fastest GT cars of their era — think McLaren F1 GTR, Mercedes CLK GTR, Porsche 911 GT1."},{"t":"GTP Class","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-gtp-class","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Car Classes · Grand Touring Prototype — IMSA's top class through the 1980s and early 90s. Unlike GT classes, GTP cars were purpose-built racing prototypes not derived from road cars. The Porsche 962 and Jaguar XJR-9 are famous GTP cars. They dominated IMSA the way F1 cars dominate their own world."},{"t":"LMP1 / LMP2","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-lmp1-lmp2","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Car Classes · Le Mans Prototype classes. LMP1 was the absolute top class — the fastest, most technologically advanced racing cars in the world during the hybrid era (Audi R18, Porsche 919, Toyota TS050). LMP2 is a spec prototype class one step below, used as a stepping stone for professional drivers."},{"t":"Hypercar Class","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-hypercar-class","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Car Classes · The top class in WEC since 2021, replacing LMP1. Designed to bring more manufacturers back to the top of endurance racing with lower costs and Balance of Performance. Cars are either Le Mans Hypercars (LMH) or LMDh prototypes that can also race in IMSA. Ferrari, Porsche, Toyota, Cadillac, BMW and Peugeot "},{"t":"GT3 Class","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-gt3-class","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Car Classes · The customer racing class introduced in 2006. GT3 cars are production-based supercars (Porsche 911, Ferrari 488, Audi R8, Mercedes-AMG GT3) modified for racing and sold to private teams. All GT3 cars are balanced through BoP (Balance of Performance) to ensure close racing regardless of manufacturer. GT3 i"},{"t":"GT4 Class","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-gt4-class","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Car Classes · A more affordable customer racing class below GT3, introduced in 2007. GT4 cars have less power (around 400hp) and fewer modifications than GT3, making them perfect for amateur racers and developing drivers. Think of GT4 as the entry point to professional GT racing."},{"t":"Balance of Performance (BoP)","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-balance-of-performance-bop","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"GT3 Terms · The equalization system that keeps all GT3 manufacturers competitive. Race organizers adjust each car's weight, engine power, fuel capacity, and aerodynamics to ensure no single manufacturer has an unfair advantage. BoP is updated throughout the season based on race results, creating the closest racing in m"},{"t":"Pro-Am / Silver Cup","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-pro-am-silver-cup","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"GT3 Terms · A driver category system in GT3 racing. Professional factory drivers are rated Platinum or Gold. Amateur gentlemen racers are rated Silver or Bronze. Pro-Am classes require one professional and one amateur per car, creating opportunities for wealthy privateers to race alongside the world's best drivers."},{"t":"Gentleman Driver","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-gentleman-driver","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"GT3 Terms · A wealthy amateur racer who purchases a GT3 car and hires professional drivers as teammates. Gentleman drivers fund their own racing programs and compete in Pro-Am classes. Some are genuinely fast (despite their amateur rating), while others simply love racing and have the budget to live the dream."},{"t":"24h Series / GT World Challenge","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-24h-series-gt-world-challenge","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"GT3 Terms · The major GT3 championship series worldwide. The GT World Challenge (formerly Blancpain GT Series) crowns European, Asian, and American champions. The 24H Series runs endurance races at circuits like Spa, Nurburgring, and Dubai. These championships are where GT3's best drivers and teams compete for glory."},{"t":"Sprint Race","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-sprint-race","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Race Formats · A short-distance race typically lasting 60-90 minutes, where outright speed matters more than tyre management or fuel strategy. F1 races are sprint format — drivers push flat-out for the entire duration. Opposite of endurance racing."},{"t":"Endurance Racing","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-endurance-racing","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Race Formats · Races measured in hours rather than laps . The winner is the car that covers the most distance within the time limit. Drivers must manage tyre wear, fuel consumption and mechanical stress over an entire day (or night). Strategy, reliability and driver changes are just as important as outright speed."},{"t":"Multi-Class Racing","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-multi-class-racing","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Race Formats · When different categories of cars race simultaneously on the same track. At Le Mans, LMP1 prototypes, LMP2 prototypes and GT cars all race at the same time. Each class has its own winner, but all cars share the same asphalt, which means a slower GT car and a much faster prototype must navigate around eac"},{"t":"Downforce","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-downforce","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Technical Terms · Aerodynamic force that pushes the car downward onto the track, generated by wings, splitters and the car's underbody. More downforce = more grip through corners = faster cornering speeds. The trade-off is drag, which reduces top speed on straights. Racing cars spend enormous effort finding the right b"},{"t":"Homologation","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-homologation","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Technical Terms · The official approval process that makes a racing car legal to compete. For GT1, a manufacturer had to build a minimum number of road-going versions of the car before the race version was approved. This is why the McLaren F1 GTR, CLK GTR and Porsche 911 GT1 all have road car counterparts — they had to"},{"t":"Hybrid Power","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-hybrid-power","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Technical Terms · A system where a car uses both a traditional combustion engine and an electric motor . In LMP1 racing, cars like the Porsche 919 and Toyota TS050 used hybrid systems that recovered energy under braking and deployed it as extra power out of corners. These systems produced over 1,000 combined horsepower"},{"t":"Monaco","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-monaco","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Famous Circuits · The Monaco Grand Prix — F1's most prestigious and glamorous race, held on the tight, narrow streets of Monte Carlo. Overtaking is nearly impossible, making qualifying crucial. Winning Monaco is considered the ultimate achievement in F1. Ayrton Senna won here six times, more than any other driver."},{"t":"Spa-Francorchamps","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-spa-francorchamps","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Famous Circuits · The legendary Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps in Belgium — one of the most challenging and revered tracks in motorsport. Famous for Eau Rouge, a high-speed uphill corner that tests driver bravery. At 7km long, Spa rewards commitment, car balance, and fearlessness."},{"t":"La Sarthe","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-la-sarthe","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Famous Circuits · The official name of the Le Mans circuit — Circuit de la Sarthe is named after the Sarthe departement (region) in northwest France where it's located. It's a semi-permanent circuit, meaning most of it uses public roads that are closed off for race week, with only a small purpose-built section. It meas"},{"t":"The Brickyard","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-the-brickyard","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Famous Circuits · The nickname of the Indianapolis Motor Speedway . In 1909 the original track was paved with 3.2 million bricks. Today it is asphalt apart from a one-yard strip of the original bricks at the start/finish line, called the \"Yard of Bricks\". Winning drivers traditionally kneel down and kiss it. The 4.023 "},{"t":"Purple Sector","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-purple-sector","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Timing & Sectors · A purple sector time means that lap segment is the fastest anyone has set all session — the absolute best time across every driver. You'll see it on timing screens and TV overlays. Only one driver can hold the purple at any given moment, making it the benchmark everyone is chasing."},{"t":"Red Sector","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-red-sector","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Timing & Sectors · A red sector means the lap or sector time has been invalidated — most commonly because the driver exceeded track limits by going beyond the white lines at a corner. Any lap with a red sector is deleted from the timing sheets and does not count toward qualifying or fastest lap records."},{"t":"Green Sector","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-green-sector","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Timing & Sectors · A green sector means the driver has beaten their own personal best time through that section of the lap. It doesn't mean they're the fastest overall (that's purple), but they are improving on themselves — a good sign they're extracting more from the car or conditions are getting better."},{"t":"Yellow Sector","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-yellow-sector","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Timing & Sectors · A yellow sector means the driver's time through that part of the lap is slower than their own personal best for that sector. It's a sign they lost time there compared to their reference lap — whether from a mistake, traffic, tyre degradation, or a cautious approach through a corner."},{"t":"🏁 Chequered Flag","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-chequered-flag","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · The most iconic flag in motorsport. Waved at the finish line to signal the end of a race, qualifying session, or practice. When the leader sees the chequered flag, the race is over. All other drivers complete their current lap and the results are declared official."},{"t":"🟡 Yellow Flag","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-yellow-flag","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · Signals danger ahead — usually a stopped car, debris, or an incident on track. Drivers must slow down and are strictly forbidden from overtaking under yellow. A double yellow (two flags waved together) means be prepared to stop. Ignoring a yellow flag results in a time penalty or disqualification."},{"t":"🔴 Red Flag","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-red-flag","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · The session is stopped immediately . All drivers must slow down and return to the pit lane. Red flags are shown when conditions are too dangerous to continue — a serious accident, heavy rain making the track undriveable, or debris that requires clearing. Races can be restarted once the issue is resolved."},{"t":"🔵 Blue Flag","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-blue-flag","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · Shown to a driver about to be lapped by a faster car. It's a warning to let the leading car past without impeding it. In F1, a driver shown three blue flags without moving aside faces a penalty. Blue flags are one of the most debated topics in motorsport — especially when backmarkers hold up title conten"},{"t":"🟢 Green Flag","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-green-flag","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · Signals that the track is clear and racing can resume — typically shown after a yellow flag period, safety car, or at the start of a session. When drivers see the green flag, full racing speed and overtaking are permitted again. At race starts it's also used to release cars from the pit lane."},{"t":"🟠 Orange Flag (Meatball)","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-orange-flag-meatball","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · Also known as the meatball flag — a black flag with an orange disc in the centre. It's shown alongside a car number to tell that specific driver they have a mechanical problem and must pit immediately for repairs. The name comes from the circular orange shape resembling a meatball."},{"t":"⬛ Black Flag","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-black-flag","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · The most severe flag shown to an individual driver — it means disqualification . Shown alongside the car number, it orders that driver to return to the pits immediately and retire from the race. Reasons include dangerous driving, ignoring penalties, or a serious rules violation. Once black-flagged, the r"},{"t":"⬜ White Flag","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-white-flag","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Racing Flags · The white flag has two different meanings depending on the series. In Formula 1 , it warns drivers that a slow-moving staff or medical vehicle is on track — drivers must be alert and prepared to avoid it. In IMSA and other endurance/sportscar series , it signals the final lap of the race , letting driver"},{"t":"NASCAR","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-nascar","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · The National Association for Stock Car Auto Racing , founded in 1948. Its top division, the Cup Series, races heavy, powerful \"stock cars\" mostly on oval tracks, and it is the most-watched form of motorsport in the United States."},{"t":"Super GT","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-super-gt","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · Japan's top grand touring championship , founded in 1993 as the JGTC. Two classes race together: GT500 factory cars from Toyota, Nissan and Honda, and the large GT300 class for GT3 and Japanese-built cars."},{"t":"DTM","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-dtm","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Series & Championships · The Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters , Germany's famous touring car series. It began in 1984 with modified production cars, later raced its own high-tech silhouette cars, and has used GT3 cars since 2021."},{"t":"Superspeedway","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-superspeedway","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"NASCAR Terms · An oval track two miles or longer , like Daytona and Talladega. Cars run flat out in huge packs, and NASCAR limits engine power there for safety, first with restrictor plates from 1988 and later with tapered spacers."},{"t":"Drafting","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-drafting","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"NASCAR Terms · Driving right behind another car to use its slipstream. Two cars nose-to-tail are faster than one alone, so drivers team up in lines, then try to slingshot past at the end. On superspeedways, drafting decides almost every race."},{"t":"The Big One","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-the-big-one","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"NASCAR Terms · The nickname for the huge multi-car crash that often happens at Daytona and Talladega. With 40 cars running inches apart at around 200 mph, one small mistake can collect a dozen cars or more."},{"t":"Stage Racing","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-stage-racing","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"NASCAR Terms · Since 2017, most Cup races are split into three stages . The top ten at the end of the first two stages earn bonus points, which rewards drivers for racing hard all day, not only at the finish."},{"t":"GT500 / GT300","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-gt500-gt300","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Super GT Terms · Super GT's two classes . GT500 is the top class of factory-built cars with about 600+ horsepower. GT300 is a bigger, more varied class including GT3 cars. Both race at the same time, so GT500 drivers must constantly lap GT300 traffic."},{"t":"Success Ballast","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-success-ballast","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Super GT Terms · Extra weight added to cars that score points , to stop one team from running away with the title. The more you score, the heavier your car becomes, until the ballast is removed for the final round."},{"t":"Class One","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-class-one","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"DTM Terms · A set of shared technical rules created by the DTM and Super GT so their cars could race each other. It used 2.0-litre turbocharged four-cylinder engines and was fully adopted by both series by 2020."},{"t":"Touring Car","k":"Term","p":"terms.html","h":"term-touring-car","s":"Racing Terms","c":"var(--gold)","x":"DTM Terms · A racing car based on a road-going saloon or coupé , like the Mercedes 190E or Alfa Romeo 155. Touring car racing is famous for close, contact-heavy battles because the cars look like what fans drive every day."},{"t":"Did You Know? Quiz","k":"Page","p":"quiz.html","h":"","s":"Quiz","c":"var(--gold)","x":"Ten questions, all answered somewhere on this site. How much did you learn?"},{"t":"Sources & Credits","k":"Page","p":"sources.html","h":"","s":"Sources","c":"var(--gold)","x":"The facts on this site were checked against the references below. Records were last checked in October 2026, when the F1, NASCAR, WEC, IMSA, Super GT and…"}];
